Subject: dmaengine: ti: k3-udma: do not close a packet per period on BCDMA cyclic RX

Symptom
  An McASP TDM capture over a BCDMA DEV_TO_MEM cyclic channel stops
  after exactly one period, whatever the period size. The RX channel
  takes one TR event and one ring completion, then goes quiet while the
  McASP RX FIFO keeps filling; ALSA times out in wait_for_avail and the
  application gets EIO.

Root cause
  Two places make the PDMA close a packet on every period of an RX
  cyclic transfer: udma_prep_dma_cyclic_tr() sets CPPI5_TR_CSF_EOP on
  the last TR of each period, and udma_configure_statictr() programs
  STATIC_TR_Z with a burst count of one period's worth of elements. On
  DEV_TO_MEM that packet boundary retires the cyclic TR descriptor,
  which the hardware would otherwise reload forever. Nothing re-arms
  it: udma_start_desc() pushes the TR descriptor to the ring exactly
  once, and the only re-arm path, udma_cyclic_packet_elapsed(), is
  host-descriptor code that would reinterpret the TR descriptor header
  and index hwdesc[] past its single entry.

Fix
  Keep EOP and the static burst count for MEM_TO_DEV, where the packet
  end is what produces the teardown completion message, and drop both
  on RX. Make udma_cyclic_packet_elapsed() a no-op outside packet mode,
  so a TR mode cyclic channel can never take the host-descriptor path.

Verified
  PocketBeagle 2 (AM6254), McASP0 TDM8 capture, kernel 7.1.0-tdm8-pb2:

    arecord -D hw:0,0 -c8 -f S32_LE -r48000 \
            --period-size=1024 --buffer-size=16384 -d3 /tmp/c.wav

  returns 0 and writes 4608044 bytes (a 44 byte WAV header plus
  3 s x 48000 frames x 8 ch x 4 bytes), and the RX channel's line in
  /proc/interrupts advances by about 140 over the run. Without this
  patch the same command fails with "read error: Input/output error"
  and that line advances by exactly 1.

Known residue
  With this patch applied, stopping a capture still logs one pair of
  lines every time:

    ti-udma 485c0100.dma-controller: chan1 teardown timeout!
    davinci-mcasp 2b00000.audio-controller: unhandled rx event. rxstat: 0x00000104

  They appear at stream stop only, never while a stream runs, and the
  next capture still opens and runs to full length, so capture is not
  affected.

  What the driver source does say: udma_synchronize() waits one second
  for teardown_completed, which udma_ring_irq_handler() signals only
  when a teardown completion message (cppi5_desc_is_tdcm()) is popped
  off the completion ring, and on DMA_DEV_TO_MEM udma_stop() asks the
  peer PDMA for that teardown. So the warning means no such message
  arrived in time. The same branch then calls udma_reset_chan(uc,
  true), a forced reset, which is why the channel is usable again. The
  McASP line is davinci_mcasp_rx_irq_handler() warning about a status
  it does not handle: it handles only ROVRN (RXSTAT bit 0), which is
  clear in 0x104; bit 8 is XRERR and bit 2 has no name in
  davinci-mcasp.h.

  What it does not say: what produces the teardown completion message
  for a cyclic RX channel. The comment this patch edits states only
  that EOP is what produces it for TX. Whether the timeout predates
  this patch is also unknown, because before it a capture never ran
  long enough to be stopped normally. The residue is therefore
  unexplained, and this patch should not be taken as complete.

Upstream status: not submitted

diff --git a/drivers/dma/ti/k3-udma.c b/drivers/dma/ti/k3-udma.c
index c964ebfcf..f03cd18c3 100644
--- a/drivers/dma/ti/k3-udma.c
+++ b/drivers/dma/ti/k3-udma.c
@@ -1043,6 +1043,10 @@ static void udma_cyclic_packet_elapsed(struct udma_chan *uc)
 	struct udma_desc *d = uc->desc;
 	struct cppi5_host_desc_t *h_desc;
 
+	/* TR mode cyclic reloads in hardware and has a single hwdesc */
+	if (!uc->config.pkt_mode)
+		return;
+
 	h_desc = d->hwdesc[d->desc_idx].cppi5_desc_vaddr;
 	cppi5_hdesc_reset_to_original(h_desc);
 	udma_push_to_ring(uc, d->desc_idx);
@@ -3209,15 +3213,11 @@ static int udma_configure_statictr(struct udma_chan *uc, struct udma_desc *d,
 		   uc->config.dir == DMA_DEV_TO_MEM &&
 		   uc->cyclic) {
 		/*
-		 * For cyclic mode with BCDMA we have to set EOP in each TR to
-		 * prevent short packet errors seen on channel teardown. So the
-		 * PDMA must close the packet after every TR transfer by setting
-		 * burst count equal to the number of bytes transferred.
+		 * A packet must not be closed per period on RX: the packet end
+		 * retires the infinitely reloading cyclic TR descriptor, which
+		 * is never re-armed, so the transfer stops after period 0.
 		 */
-		struct cppi5_tr_type1_t *tr_req = d->hwdesc[0].tr_req_base;
-
-		d->static_tr.bstcnt =
-			(tr_req->icnt0 * tr_req->icnt1) / dev_width;
+		d->static_tr.bstcnt = 0;
 	} else {
 		d->static_tr.bstcnt = 0;
 	}
@@ -3524,13 +3524,17 @@ udma_prep_dma_cyclic_tr(struct udma_chan *uc, dma_addr_t buf_addr,
 	 * For BCDMA <-> PDMA transfers, the EOP flag needs to be set on the
 	 * last TR of a descriptor, to mark the packet as complete.
 	 * This is required for getting the teardown completion message in case
-	 * of TX, and to avoid short-packet error in case of RX.
+	 * of TX.
 	 *
 	 * As we are in cyclic mode, we do not know which period might be the
 	 * last one, so set the flag for each period.
+	 *
+	 * Never on RX: closing a packet per period retires the reloading
+	 * cyclic TR descriptor after period 0 and nothing re-arms it.
 	 */
 	if (uc->config.ep_type == PSIL_EP_PDMA_XY &&
-	    uc->ud->match_data->type == DMA_TYPE_BCDMA) {
+	    uc->ud->match_data->type == DMA_TYPE_BCDMA &&
+	    dir == DMA_MEM_TO_DEV) {
 		period_csf = CPPI5_TR_CSF_EOP;
 	}
 
